In article <9bc9c9cd.0402260631.3ca837fa@posting.google.com>,
Claude <claude.plouffe@ec.gc.ca> wrote:
> I would like to know if it is possible to reformat the output
> generated by the last command?

The "last" command dumps out records from the file "/var/adm/wtmp". These
records are in "utmp" format.

% man utmp
[...]
  The utmp file	records	information about who is currently using the system.
  The file is a	sequence of utmp entries, as defined in	struct utmp in the
  utmp.h file.
[...]
% cat /usr/include/utmp.h
[...]
struct utmp {
	char	ut_user[32];		/* User login name */
	char	ut_id[14];		/* /etc/inittab id- IDENT_LEN in init */
	char	ut_line[32];		/* device name (console, lnxx) */
	short	ut_type; 		/* type of entry */
[...]
	time_t	ut_time;		/* time entry was made */
	char	ut_host[64];		/* host name same as MAXHOSTNAMELEN */
};

> The host field is truncated to 16 char where in fact it is a 256 char
> field.  My host names are longer than 16 characters.

64 char, actually.

> Is there another command I can use (perl script maybe) ?

That should be enough to get you started.

