As has been mentioned in a previous reply, the file is most likely 
sparse.  Since this is an AdvFS file system you can do 2 things to see 
the sparseness.

        ls -s

Will tell you the amount of storage actually allocated to the file vs 
the location of the last byte of the file.

        /usr/sbin/showfile -x

will show you the extent information for the file telling you what 
storage has been allocated.  There are man pages that describe both of 
these commands and options.

There is no way to make the file smaller without rewriting it from 
scratch and eliminating the parts you don't want.  

A typical cp/tar/cpio type command will only compound your problem 
because these utilities will replace sparse holes with real storage.

There vdump/vrestore commands are suppose to know how to read the sparse 
map and copy a file without filling in the holes.  Check the examples in 
the man pages, but something like this might work for copying such a 
file:

    vdump -0f - kc.flo-2 | vrestore -xf - -D /destination/directory/

Never tried it, so I'm not sure it will do what I suggest.

The dd command also has a sparse option that attempts to avoid writing 
blocks of zeros in a file it is copying.  

        dd if kc.flo-2 of /target/directory/kc.flo-2 conv=sparse

However, dd will read every virtual byte of the file and compare it for 
zeros.  620GB of reading holes and comparing it against zeros will 
consume huge amounts of CPU time.  It would be best to use 
vdump/vrestore as a copy tool as it will read the extent map and just 
read the parts of the file that exist.

Of course there is the very rude and crude approach of writing a program 
to read the showfile -x output and only copy the parts of the file that 
have storage.  of course if you are going to do this, you might as well 
just use the fcntl( F_GETMAP ) system call and have AdvFS tell you what 
parts of the file are sparse in the first place (also works for sparse 
UFS files)

Now if your goal was to transfer the file to a different systems that is 
not NFS connected or you want to compress it first, then you might be 
able to combine a compression, tar, untar, uncompress, dd sequence, and 
vrestore was not an option on the target system, then you might try

        gzip kc.flo-2
        tar cf kc.flo-2.tar kc.flo-2.gz

on the other end

        tar xf kc.flo-2.tar
        gunzip -c kc.flo-2.gz | dd of kc.flo-2 conv=sparse

You might have to do the compression first because tar does not really 
like huge files.  Compressing it first may keep the real size to 
something tar can swallow.

I hope this helps.

                                        Bob Harris


