In article <bvo7lk$pmf$1@planja.arnes.si>,
Zlatko Rek  <no.name@spam.com> wrote:
> The disk is 20GB and the file is 620GB!

In UNIX file access is performed one of two ways, through memory
mapping or through stream I/O, both of which can produce this result
albeit through different operations.

If the file was memory mapped but unallocated and a program wrote to a
block near the end of the mapped region, or if the file was a stream
and the program performed a very large seek operation and then wrote
some data, the result would be what's called a "sparse file": a file
that is logically thousands or (in this case) millions of virtual
"null" blocks that aren't actually allocated from the pool of free
blocks on disk, with a handful of actual data blocks where writes have
actually occurred.

